## Discussion
In our previous study, we observed an ageing effect of the pure CAU-10-PDC membrane when it was exposed to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}{}^{36}$ . Specifically, when the CAU-10-PDC membrane was exposed to a mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (50/50) at a total pressure of 2 bar at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ we observed a dramatic decrease in $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ permeance over one order of magnitude in the period of $100\mathrm{min}$ . A change in space group, from $I4_{1}/a m d$ to $I4_{1}$ , was also observed for CAU-10-PDC treated with $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Herein we assessed the stability of the mixedlinker CAU-10-PDC-H membranes for the operation of gas separation (Fig. 4). The CAU-10-PDC-H (7:3) membrane presents excellent stability for the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ as well as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Specifically, this membrane’s gas permeability and selectivity barely change during the experiment. Interestingly, a slight decrease in the permeability of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ less than two times, is found for the membranes of CAU-10-PDC-H (5:5) and CAU-10- PDC-H (7:3), which is far less pronounced than the previous findings for the pure CAU-10-PDC membranes. The results suggest that incorporating the BDC linker into the framework of CAU-10-PDC makes this membrane more robust for gas separation.
In summary, we studied the origin of the structural deformation of MOF membranes under exposure to gases. Pure CAU-10-PDC membrane has a high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation factor (62). However, the strong interaction between the PDC linker and the adsorbate molecules $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ ) leads to a significant structural change in CAU-10-PDC. The structural deformation was investigated by the in situ characterizations with XRD and DRIFT spectroscopy. The mixed linker approach studied in this work, via mixing PDC and BDC linkers in a MOF, could engineer the aperture size and the gas adsorption properties of the CAU-10-based MOFs and suppress the flexibility observed in CAU-10-PDC. Specifically, a $30\mathrm{mol}.\%$ substitution of PDC with BDC can significantly stabilize the MOF structure during the gas adsorption and permeation. For the optimized CAU-10-PDC-H (70/30), the structural change is barely observed in the in situ analysis with XRD or DRIFT spectroscopy. The CAU-10-PDC-H (70/30) membrane presents a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{4}$ separation factor of 74.2 and a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability of 1,111.1 Barrer under 2 bar of feed pressure at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## Methods
Chemicals and materials. $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{3}{\cdot}18\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was purchased from JT Baker. Pyridine-3,5-dicarboxylic acid $(3,5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PDC}$ , $98\%$ ) and benzene-1,3-dicarboxylic acid $(1,3{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC},99\%$ ) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. Hereafter $3{,}5{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PDC}$ and $1,3\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ were respectively referred to as PDC and BDC. $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ ) and methanol (MeOH, $99\%$ ) were purchased from Macron. All chemicals were used without further purification. The deionized water (DI water) used for synthesis was purified using an ELGA VEOLIA PURELAB® classic analytical ultrapure water system. Porous $\alpha$ -alumina substrates were purchased from the CHAO YUE Diamonds Ltd. Co. The substrates were composed of $\alpha$ - alumina particles with an average particle size of approximately $400\mathrm{nm}.$ , and they possessed a diameter of $40\mathrm{mm}$ , a thickness of $2\mathrm{mm}$ , and a porosity of $34\%$ .
Synthesis of CAU-10-PDC-H powder. For the synthesis of CAU-10-PDC, PDC $\mathrm{\langle}5\mathrm{mmol}\rangle$ ) was added to DMF $(6\mathrm{ml})$ . The mixture was sonicated using an ultrasonication bath until the solid content was completely dissolved. A separate solution was prepared by dissolving $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{3}{\cdot}18\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (5 mmol) in DI water $(24~\mathrm{ml})$ ). The two solutions mentioned above were then mixed and refluxed under agitation at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 days. MOF particles formed during the reaction. The solvent was removed from the suspension via vacuum filtration. The solid product was dispersed in methanol and agitated at room temperature for 1 day, which allowed for the removal of DMF or water as guest molecules in the MOF. Vacuum filtration was applied for the removal of methanol. The powder sample was dried in a convection oven at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 day.
The synthesis of CAU-10-H resembled that of CAU-10-PDC, except that PDC $\mathrm{\nabla}5\mathrm{mmol}$ ) was replaced by BDC (5 mmol). The synthesis of mixed-linker CAU-10- PDC-H (7:3) also resembled the synthesis of pure CAU-10-PDC, except that PDC ( $3.5\mathrm{mmol};$ and BDC $\left(1.5\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ ) were used in the first step. The synthesis of mixed-linker CAU-10-PDC-H (5:5) or CAU-10-PDC-H (3:7) was conducted in the aforementioned manner; and the quantity of PDC and BDC used in the synthesis was respectively (2.5 and $2.5\:\mathrm{mmol}$ ) or (1.5 and $3.5\mathrm{mmol}$ ).
Deposition of CAU-10-PDC-H seed layer. Prior to deposition, the $\alpha$ -alumina substrate was immersed in DI water and cleaned using an ultrasonication bath for $24\mathrm{h}$ and then dried in a convection oven at $105^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for at least $24\mathrm{h}$ . The powder of CAU-10-PDC, CAU-10-H, or mixed-linker CAU-10-PDC-H was dispersed in DI water to form a $0.2\mathrm{wt\%}$ suspension. Approximately $2.5\mathrm{ml}$ of the suspension was applied dropwise onto the substrate followed by spin-on deposition at $2000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{s}$ . The spin-on deposition was performed using a Laurell spin coater (ModelWS-650M2-23NPPB). The sample was placed in a convection oven at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ . The deposition procedure mentioned above was repeated for two more times on the same substrate to increase the coverage of the MOF seed layer.
Time-resolved XRD was performed at Taiwan Photon Source (TPS) 19 A station at the National Synchrotron Radiation Research Center (NSRRC). The X-ray at a wavelength of 0.77489 Å $(16\mathrm{keV})$ was generated from a cryogenic undulator under vacuum (CU15). The proprietary setup for the measurements can be found in our previous report36. The CAU-10-PDC-H powder samples were packed in a capillary tube with a diameter of $0.7\mathrm{mm}$ . Prior to the measurement, the powder sample underwent degassing at 0.005 bar at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for at least $30\mathrm{min}$ . The sample was then cooled to $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at 2 bar was introduced to the capillary tube with the MOF sample. XRD patterns were continuously recorded at intervals of $12\mathrm{min}$ over a time span of up to $120\mathrm{min}$ . Each diffraction pattern was recorded using a MYTHEN $18\mathrm{K}$ position-sensitive detector with exposure duration of 1 s.
Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of MOF powder and membrane samples were acquired using a BRUKER ALPHA II FTIR spectrometer equipped with a KBr beam splitter. The measurements were conducted in the mode of attenuated total reflectance with a diamond crystal. Each spectrum was recorded from 120 scans at a spectral resolution of $4\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The PDC-to-BDC ratios of the CAU-10-PDC-H powder and membrane samples were derived from the peak areas at $770\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $\bar{7}22\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ for PDC and BDC, respectively.
$^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra were acquired using a Bruker AVIII ${500}\mathrm{MHz}$ NMR equipped with a cryo prodigy broadband probe. A missed solvent with $600\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $10\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of $40\mathrm{wt\%}$ NaOD in $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was used for dissolving the powder sample in order to form a homogenous liquid for the measurement.
Elemental analysis (EA) of CHN was performed using an Elementar vario EL cube analyzer (for CHNS). First, samples were placed into a tin capsule and then in the autosampler to inject a high-temperature furnace. Secondly, samples were burned in the oxygen flow at temperature of up to $1800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . And then, the gases $(\Nu_{2}$ , $\mathrm{N_{x}O_{y},}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{SO}_{3};$ ) formed during the combustion were passed through a reduction tube to produce a gas mixture $(\Nu_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{SO}_{2}^{'}$ . The gas mixture was then passed through the adsorption column to separate the different gases. Finally, the gas composition was analyzed using a gas chromatograph equipped with a thermal conductivity detector. Note that $\Nu_{2}$ bypassed the adsorption column and was detected directly. The analysis was used acetanilide standard in the CHN module with $<0.1\%$ abs. for each element. Considering the composition of CAU-10-PDC-H, we set the molecular formula as $[\mathrm{Al(OH)(BDC)}_{x}(\mathrm{PDC)}_{(1-x)}]{\cdot}y\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}z\mathrm{DMF}$ , for the measurements.
A Hitachi S4800 field emission scanning electron microscope (SEM) was used to characterize the morphology of the membrane samples. All samples were placed in vacuum desiccator overnight to remove moisture. Prior to imaging, the samples were coated with platinum via sputtering deposition under acceleration voltage of $25\mathrm{V}$ for $40{\mathrm{~s.}}$ SEM was performed under an acceleration voltage of $10\mathrm{kV}$ during image acquisition.
Gas adsorption isotherms of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ were measured at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the pressure decay method using a homemade device72. CAU-10-PDC-H powder samples were inserted into a Swagelok® filter element kit and wrapped loosely with aluminum foil. They were loaded into the sample chamber and degassed at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight before each measurement to remove any gases trapped in the sample73. The adsorption isotherms of all the samples were fitted by Langmuir model.
Nitrogen adsorption isotherms were obtained using a Micromeritics (ASAP2020) at $77\mathrm{K}$ Before the measurement, approximately $\mathbf{0.l-0.3\:g}$ of powder sample was placed in a tube and degassed under 0.005 mbar at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Combination of the excess sorption work (ESW) and the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method was utilized to receive a more accurate surface area of the material57,58.
The increase of the downstream pressure as a function of time was then converted into the gas permeability for the membrane using the following equation:
$$
{\mathrm{Permeability}}={\frac{V}{R T A}}\left({\frac{\mathrm{d}p}{\mathrm{d}t}}\right){\frac{l}{\triangle p}}
$$
where $R$ is the gas constant, $T$ is temperature, $A$ is the membrane area that allows for permeation, $V$ is the volume of the downstream reservoir, $\textstyle{\frac{\mathrm{d}{\boldsymbol{p}}}{\mathrm{d}t}}$ is the pressure on the permeate side as a function of time, $l$ is the thickness of membrane and $\Delta p$ is the transmembrane pressure difference. The ideal selectivity of one gas species over another was calculated as the ratio of the permeability of these two species obtained from their single-gas permeation tests. The effective membrane area used in for Eq. (1) was measured by a photographic image of the membrane sample with the aid of an open-source package, Image $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}^{75}$ .
The same setup was used for the mixed-gas permeation tests, and these tests were conducted in a very similar manner to the single-gas permeation. The feed gas was composed of either $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (50/50 in mol) or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (50/50 in mol) at a total pressure of 2 bar at $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The gas composition on the product side was analyzed using a gas chromatograph (Shimadzu GC-2014) equipped with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD) and a Shincarbon-ST column. The separation factor of species $i$ over $j$ was computed using the following equation:
$$
\mathrm{Separation~factor}=\frac{\ensuremath{\gamma}_{i}/\ensuremath{\gamma}_{j}}{\ensuremath{x}_{i}/\ensuremath{x}_{j}}
$$
where $x_{i}$ and $y_{i}$ are the molar fraction of $i$ on the feed and on the permeate side, respectively; and $x_{j}$ and $y_{j}$ are the molar fraction of $j$ on the feed and on the permeate side, respectively
Computational methods. Structure relaxation of CAU-10-PDC-H structures were implemented using CASTEP module of Materials Studio suite76. The calculation was used in reciprocal space as pseudopotential representation. The exchangecorrelations were applied using Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) functional in the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) of the plane wave pseudopotential method77. Ultrasoft pseudopotential was conducted to calculate the interactions between the ionic nucleus and valence electrons78. Broyden–Fletcher–Goldfarb–Shanno algorithm was utilized for geometry optimization79. The value of cut-off energy was set at $340\mathrm{eV}$ .
PLD and PSD of CAU-10-PDC-H were both estimated by using an open-source package, $\mathrm{Zeo}++80\$ , based on the CIF files corresponding to the optimized structures. Note that PSDs were implemented using a total of 50,000 Monte Carlo (MC) samples per unit cell with a probe radius of $\mathrm{\check{\Omega}}_{1.1\mathrm{~\AA~}}\mathrm{\&~}\mathrm{\Omega}$ .
In the vibrational frequency analysis, the crystallographic structures of CAU10-PDC and deformed CAU-10-PDC were used to determine the positions of all the atoms in the MOF models. Periodic density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed using the PBE exchange-correlation functional within the generalized gradient approach $\mathrm{(GGA)^{77}}$ , as implemented in VASP $5.4.4^{82}$ . The valence density was expanded in a plane wave basis set with a kinetic energy cutoff $450\mathrm{eV}$ , where the effect of core electrons on valence density was considered using the projector-augmented wave method $({\mathrm{PAW}})^{83}$ . A $2\times2\times4$ Monkhorst–Pack k point mesh84 was used for integration over the Brillouin zone in reciprocal space for both geometry optimization and frequency calculations.
For the energy decomposition analysis $(\dot{\mathrm{EDA}})^{85}$ , the PBE exchangecorrelation functional77 with semiempirical dispersion corrections from the DFT- $\mathrm{D}3^{86}$ method was used for geometry optimizations and single point calculations, as implemented in the Quickstep module of CP2K package87. Double- $\zeta$ valence plus polarization MOLOPT basis $\mathsf{s e t}^{88-91}$ , in conjunction with the relativistic, norm-conserving Goedecker–Teter–Hutter pseudopotentials, was used for all atoms. The auxiliary plane-wave basis set was defined by an energy cutoff of $450\mathrm{Ry}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , accompanied by the relative cutoff of $60\mathrm{Ry}$ for the Gaussian basis set collocation. The binding energy $(E_{\mathrm{bind}})$ between the guest molecule and the MOF system can be defined as:
$$
E_{\mathrm{bind}}=E\big(H\bullet G_{\mathrm{def}}\big)-E(H)-E(G)
$$
where $E\left(H\bullet G_{\mathrm{def}}\right)$ corresponds to the energy of the deformed MOF-guest complex, and $E(H)$ and $E(G)$ represent the energies of the original MOF and guest molecule, respectively. In order to obtain a comprehensive understanding of the conformational change, an EDA was performed. Within the EDA framework, the $E\left(H\bullet G_{\mathrm{def}}\right)$ can be further decomposed as:
$$
E\big(H\bullet G_{\mathrm{def}}\big)=E(H)+E(G)+E_{\mathrm{def}}+E_{\mathrm{int}}
$$
where the deformation energy $(E_{\mathrm{def}})$ refers to the energy that is required to distort the isolated MOF and guest molecule from their original geometries to the geometries they have in the deformed MOF–guest complex, and the interaction energy $(E_{\mathrm{int}})$ represents the energy difference induced by the guest–host interactions. By substituting Eq. (3) into Eq. (4), we can express the binding energy as the sum of $E_{\mathrm{def}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{int}}$ .
$$
E_{\mathrm{bind}}=E_{\mathrm{def}}+E_{\mathrm{int}}
$$
